<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carlisle</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Luguvalium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carlisle is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a>, north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, near the Scottish border, on the site of the Roman town of Luguvalium close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>. It was the centre of the civitas of the Carvetii and later a stronghold disputed between England and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, with a castle and a cathedral founded in the 12th century. It is the main town of Cumbria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carlisle.jpg" alt="Carlisle" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bill Harrison · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carlisle_Market_Cross_6310907_2233337d.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Carlisle lies on a slight rise at the confluence of the rivers Eden, Caldew and Petteril, about eight miles (13 km) south of the present Anglo-Scottish border, which earned it the nickname of the Great Border City. Its name comes from the Cumbric Caer Luel. Luguvalium took its name from a native name reconstructed as Brittonic Luguwaljon, the city of Luguwalos, a Celtic given name meaning strength of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugus">Lugus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest recorded inhabitants were the Carvetii. Luguvalium was founded to serve the forts along <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>, though it may have earlier origins. After Roman rule the region was the British kingdom of Rheged, whose capital, associated with Urien and his son Owain, has been identified with the Cair Ligualid listed by Nennius. Rheged came under Northumbrian control before 730, and for the rest of the first millennium Carlisle was contested between the kingdom of Strathclyde and Anglian Northumbria. In 685 St Cuthbert was shown its walls and a Roman fountain.</p><p>A priory built in the early 12th century became Carlisle Cathedral in 1133, when the Diocese of Carlisle was created, and as a diocesan seat the town gained city status. It was county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumberland">Cumberland</a>, and its earliest recorded charter was issued by Henry II.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-queen-of-scots">Mary, Queen of Scots</a> was held in the castle in 1568. In 1539 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> employed the engineer Stefan von Haschenperg to modernise the defences. Carlisle has been besieged at least ten times, and the reiving of the border families was suppressed more firmly after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a> became James I of England in 1603. In the 19th century textile mills and engineering works opened in the Caldew Valley and Carlisle became a railway town, with seven companies sharing its station.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Carlisle is the only city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a> and its largest settlement, with a population of 77,730 in 2021. The castle houses the Border Regiment Museum, and Tullie House Museum, opened in 1893, shows the Roman occupation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a> and the Border Reivers. The cathedral remains the seat of the Diocese of Carlisle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carlisle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The route lay slightly north of the Stanegate, an important Roman road built several decades earlier to link two forts that guarded river crossings: Corstopitum (Corbridge) on the Tyne and <b>Luguvalium</b> (Carlisle) on the Eden.</blockquote><a href="/work/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the other, the chorus gives the last words of a Jacobite rebel before his execution, perhaps at <b>Carlisle</b>, where dozens of rebels were executed: seeing his lover at the gallows, he tells her they will meet again in Scotland, though by different roads.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-bonnie-banks-o-loch-lomond">The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond</a></li><li><blockquote>The Carvetii lived in the Eden valley and around <b>Carlisle</b> in what is now Cumbria.</blockquote><a href="/state/carvetii">Carvetii</a></li><li><blockquote>Its location is uncertain, but scholars usually place it around <b>Carlisle</b>, Cumbria and Galloway.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-rheged">Kingdom of Rheged</a></li></ul></section>
